    desc: "<h2>How to Use It</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Enter the wall area (m²)</strong>. Type the exact number and the tool recalculates on the fly — no button to press, no page reload. This value is the core input the formula operates on.</li>\n<li><strong>Enter the roll coverage (m²)</strong>. Type the exact number and the tool recalculates on the fly — no button to press, no page reload. This value is the core input the formula operates on.</li>\n<li><strong>Read the result panel</strong>. The answer appears instantly, and the step-by-step breakdown underneath shows every stage of the math so you can verify the output yourself.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjust and compare</strong>. Change any input and watch the result move. Use the batch mode to run several scenarios side by side before deciding.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>What Each Input Means</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wall Area (m²)</strong> — the numeric value the formula uses for that part of the calculation. Keep the units consistent with the label; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>Roll Coverage (m²)</strong> — the numeric value the formula uses for that part of the calculation. Keep the units consistent with the label; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The Formula Behind the Result</h3>\n<p>At its core this is the textbook construction calculation: take your primary value, apply the rate or ratio inputs, then adjust for any optional factors you enabled. The breakdown underneath shows each stage and follows the standard material yields and wastage factors used by contractors.</p>\n<h3>Worked Example</h3>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with Wall Area (m²) of 30, Roll Coverage (m²) of 5, this tool returns <strong>Rolls: 7</strong>.</p><p>Here is how it gets there:</p><ul><li>Rolls = area / coverage = 30 / 5 = 6</li><li>Add 15% waste = 7 rolls</li></ul><p>Work through the same steps with your own values and you will land on the identical result — that is the value of a calculator that shows its working instead of hiding it.</p>\n\n<h2>Key Advantages Over a Spreadsheet</h2>\n<p>Plenty of calculators can give you a number. This free wallpaper calculator is built for people who also want to trust the number: the working is always visible, the tool never phones home, and nothing is locked behind a sign-up. Compared to a spreadsheet, it saves you the formula setup; compared to a generic tool site, it gives you the derivation instead of a black box; compared to an app, it needs nothing to install.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Client-side only</strong> — your numbers never leave your device. No uploads, no logs, no tracking.</li><li><strong>Works offline</strong> — after the first visit the tool caches locally and runs without an internet connection.</li><li><strong>Free forever</strong> — no premium tiers, no hidden charges, no usage caps of any kind.</li><li><strong>Step-by-step output</strong> — every calculator shows its working, not just the answer.</li><li><strong>Accessible</strong> — keyboard navigation, screen-reader friendly, and reduced-motion support built in.</li><li><strong>Consistent across 543+ tools</strong> — the same clean workflow on every calculator in the collection.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>When You Might Need This</h2>\n<p>Students use Wallpaper Calculator to check homework and, more importantly, to understand how each input moves the result — that is how the formula sticks. Professionals rely on it during client meetings for quick estimates they can defend line by line. Shoppers and planners plug in their own numbers to compare options and set realistic budgets before they commit. Even casual users reach for it when they want certainty without the effort, and the breakdown doubles as a quick refresher on how the math works.</p>\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2><p>People usually trip on three things with Wallpaper Calculator: using the wrong units, mixing up which field is the main input, and assuming the answer is fixed when it only reflects the numbers entered. The output is only as good as the inputs — so if the result looks surprising, walk the breakdown backwards and check each value you typed. The tool shows every stage precisely so you can find the mistake in seconds instead of guessing.</p>\n<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> if the result looks off, first check the units, then the formula steps, then your inputs — in that order. Nine times out of ten the breakdown reveals exactly where the discrepancy started.</p>\n<p>Keep the result handy if you plan to share or revisit it — use the share link, image, or CSV export to save the exact inputs and output together. That way the calculation can be reproduced later, which matters when the numbers are part of a bigger decision.</p>\n<h2>The CalcPro Philosophy</h2>\n<p>All 543+ CalcPro tools follow the same philosophy: serve the user, not the tracker. No analytics by default, no data collection, no server uploads. Every calculator runs in your browser. The code is open and readable, so you can verify exactly what the tool does with your inputs — the same standard this page holds itself to.</p>\n<p>People searching for this tool often pair it with export results, works on mobile — all of them are covered elsewhere in the CalcPro directory, free and private like this one.</p>",
